===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The condition or state being [[recrudescent]]; the condition of something (often [[undesirable]]) [[break out|breaking out]] again, or [[reemerge|re-emerging]] after [[temporary]] [[abatement]] or [[suppression]]. {{defdate|from mid 17th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|recrudency|q1=archaic|reincrudation|q2=obsolete}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Duke of Argyll [''i.e.'', {{w|George Campbell, 8th Duke of Argyll}}]|chapter=The Age of Covenants|title=Scotland as It Was and as It Is|edition=2nd|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:David Douglas (publisher)|David Douglas]]|year=1887|page=134|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/scotlandasitwasa00argy/page/134/mode/1up|oclc=1061880144|passage=The population of particular countries, or districts of country, may be given up to less improving pursuits than those of agriculture. A '''recrudescence''' of barbarism may condemn it [''i.e.'', land] to chronic poverty and waste.}}
#* {{RQ:Conrad Heart of Darkness|page=204|passage=A blinding sunlight drowned all this at times in a sudden '''recrudescence''' of glare.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Albert E. Gunn; George O. Zenner, Jr.|title=Religious Discrimination in the Selection of Medical Students: A Case Study|journal=Issues in Law and Medicine|location=Terre Haute, Ind.|publisher=National Legal Center for the Medically Dependent & Disabled and the [[w:Horatio Storer|Horatio R. Storer Foundation]]|month=spring|year=1996|volume=11|issue=4|pages=363–378|issn=8756-8160|oclc=1081170257|pmid=8934858|newversion=republished in|title2=[[w:The Linacre Quarterly|The Linacre Quarterly: Journal of the Catholic Medical Association]]|location2=Wynnewood, Pa.|publisher2=w:Catholic Medical Association|year2=August 1996|volume2=63|issue2=3|page2=52|pageurl2=https://epublications.marquette.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?&article=2084&context=lnq#page=12|doi2=10.1080/20508549.1999.11878356|issn2=0024-3639|oclc2=1588532|passage=Of course, bad habits die hard, and even with the new policy there were '''recrudescences''' of the prior practices in the following years.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James U. Van Dyke|chapter=Cues for Reproduction in Squamate Reptiles|editors=Justin L. Rheubert; Dustin S. Siegel; w:Stanley E. Trauth|title=Reproductive Biology and Phylogeny of Lizards and Tuatara|series=Reproductive Biology and Phylogeny|seriesvolume=10|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher=w:CRC Press|year=2015|page=119|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=v4zNBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA119|isbn=978-1-4665-7986-6|passage=High serum melatonin concentrations generally suppress reproduction in male squamates. Pinealectomy, which eliminates most melatonin secretion, induces testicular '''recrudescence''' in male green anoles, ''Anolis carolinensis'', in autumn, but not in summer{{nb...}}. Injected melatonin does not inhibit testicular '''recrudescence''' in pinealectomized males, but does so in intact males{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|by extension}} The [[acute#Adjective|acute]] [[recurrence]] of a [[disease]], or its [[symptom]]s, after a period of [[improvement]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J[ames] Kirkpatrick|chapter=Section II. Of the Variolous Fuel, or Internal Inherent Cause of the Small Pox.|title=The Analysis of Inoculation: Comprizing the History, Theory, and Practice of It: With an Occasional Consideration of the Most Remarkable Appearances in the Small Pox|location=London|publisher=Printed for J. Millan,{{nb...|near Charing Cross}}; J[ames] Buckland,{{nb...|in Pater-Noster-Row}}; and [[w:Ralph Griffiths|R[alph] Griffiths]],{{nb...|in St. Paul’s Church Yard.}}|year=1754|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hgM_w7fIFPcC&pg=PA34|oclc=723469781|passage=Very probably however, ſuppoſing Mr. {{smallcaps|Dwight}}'s Account to be Fact, not more than one Conſtitution in one Million is liable to ſuch repeated and diſtant '''Recrudeſcences''' of this Diſeaſe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J. L. Bardsley|entry=HYDROPHOBIA|editors=[[w:John Forbes (physician)|John Forbes]]; w:Alexander Tweedie; w:John Conolly|title=The Cyclopædia of Practical Medicine;{{nb...|Comprising Treatises on the Nature and Treatment of Diseases, Materia Medica and Therapeutics, Medical Jurisprudence, etc. etc.}}|location=London|publisher=Sherwood, Gilbert, and Piper; Baldwin and Cradock,{{nb...|Paternoster-Row}}; Whittaker, Treacher, and Co.,{{nb...|Ave-Maria-Lane.}}|year=1833|volume=II (EME–ISC)|page=484|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=umsSAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA484|column=1|oclc=1012799552|passage=In other cases the wound opens of itself, and discharges a peculiar matter. Something similar is known to take place in traumatic tetanus; and although in this affection, as well as in hydrophobia, we cannot explain why the phenomenon of '''recrudescence''' does not occur in many fatal examples, yet we ought not therefore to deny that in those cases in which it does appear, the connexion between the '''recrudescence''' and the disease is most remarkable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Alexander Gordon|chapter=Recurring Epidemics; Recrudescence|title=Notes on the Hygiene of Cholera for Ready Reference|location=London|publisher=Bailliere, Tindall, & Cox; Madras: Gantz Brothers,{{nb...|7 and 8, Mount Road}}; Bombay; Calcutta: Thacker & Co.|year=1877|section=paragraph 5|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PBgDAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA1-PA10|oclc=14858340|passage=It becomes very difficult under many circumstances to distinguish between an epidemic solely due to '''''recrudescence''''' of the cholera principle retained from previous outbreak, and an epidemic the result of fresh introduction.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} The [[production]] of a [[fresh]] [[shoot#Noun|shoot]] from a [[ripened#Adjective|ripened]] [[spike#Noun|spike]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Maxwell T. Masters|Maxwell T[ylden] Masters]]|chapter=Prolification|title=Vegetable Teratology, an Account of the Principal Deviations from the Usual Construction of Plants|series=Ray Society|seriesvolume=XLV|location=London|publisher=Published for the {{w|Ray Society}} by {{w|Robert Hardwicke}},{{nb...|192, Piccadilly.}}|year=1869|section=book I (Deviations from Ordinary Arrangement), part III (Alterations of Position)|page=104|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6cpLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA104|oclc=982524630|passage=[[w:Frédéric Kirschleger|[Frédéric] Kirschleger]] describes a tuft of leaves as occurring on the apex of the flowering spike after the maturation of the fruit in ''Plantago'', and a similar growth frequently takes place in the common wallflower, in ''Antirrhinum majus'', &c. In cases where a renewal of growth in the axis of inflorescence has taken place after the ripening of the fruit, the French botanists use the term '''recrudescence''', but the growth in question by no means always occurs after the ripening of the fruit, but frequently before.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Robert Holland|title=Monstrous Plants in 1872|editor=[[w:John Ellor Taylor|J[ohn] E[llor] Taylor]]|magazine=[[w:Science-Gossip|Hardwicke’s Science-Gossip: An Illustrated Medium of Interchange and Gossip for Students and Lovers of Nature]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Robert Hardwicke}},{{nb...|192, Piccadilly.}}|year=1872|year_published=1873|volume=VIII|issue=96|page=271|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Oa8_AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA2-PA271|column=1|oclc=1041007845|passage=A great many of the scapes have furnished examples of "'''recrudescence'''," a few flowers having been produced amongst the ripening capsules; but fresh flower-stalks have also continued to shoot up from the root, and at the time I write (Oct. 4) I see there is one very pretty bunch of flowers upon a last year's seedling plant.}}

